  "final_message": "I haven't changed anything yet. Removing `channel` from `OrderPlaced` conflicts with a project rule, so I need you to decide how to proceed.\n\n**The conflict:** ARCH-4 in `CLAUDE.md` says `OrderPlaced` is consumed by billing-service and analytics-ingest, which live in other repositories. It says \"changes are additive; renames and removals go through deprecation.\" Taking `channel` out of the event (`src/core/events.ts:16`, `:50`) is a removal. This repo can't show me whether those two services read `channel`. If either one deserializes it as required, the change breaks it in production.\n\nThe rest of the change is safe to make:\n- **`GET /orders/{id}` (ARCH-8):** this response never included `channel` (`src/api/responses.ts`), so web-storefront isn't affected.\n- **`POST /orders`:** if we stop reading `channel`, it becomes more lenient. Clients that still send it get ignored, and clients that leave it out now succeed. Nothing that works today would break.\n\n**Options:**\n\n1. **Deprecate first, remove later (what ARCH-4 calls for).** Now:\n   - Mark `OrderPlaced.channel` as `@deprecated` and keep filling it in.\n   - Make `channel` optional on `POST /orders`.\n   - Update the README and `CLAUDE.md` examples.\n   - Tell the billing-service and analytics-ingest owners.\n\n   In a later release, after the deprecation period, remove the field from the event and the model and bump `schemaVersion` to 4. The order model keeps `channel` until then (it can become optional), because the event is built from it.\n\n2. **Remove it everywhere now.** This is fine if you've already confirmed with the billing-service and analytics-ingest owners that neither reads `channel`, and Architecture accepts skipping the deprecation step. I'd remove it from the event, the model, validation, request parsing, tests and docs, and bump `OrderPlaced.schemaVersion` from 3 to 4 so consumers can see the shape changed.\n\nIf \"nothing reads channel\" already comes from those two teams, tell me and I'll do option 2. Otherwise I suggest option 1.",
